this was um  a family holiday we took to florida this  is disney  and  this is the end of sort of two weeks out  in disney and i'm completely overloaded  by this point  it was in her teens that helen began to  feel that she was different actually the  day after this photo i stayed in the  hotel for the day while my mum brother  went off to the parks again because i  just couldn't get out of bed it would be  years though before she would get the  diagnosis confirming she is autistic  living with the questions she says was  painful at the time my gp didn't know  much about autism and he basically told  me what i was experiencing was a  combination of hormones and exam stress  so sent me away in tears  helen is one of thousands of autistic  women facing these kind of barriers to  getting a diagnosis  the latest research shows the ratio of  men to women diagnosed with autism is  around three to one and the median age  of diagnosis for boys is 10 but for  girls it's 13. there are various  theories on why this is including that  girls can be better at hiding or masking  their condition and the teachers tend to  under report traits of autism in girls  tv star christine mcginnis is one of  these women  a documentary she released this week  with her husband paddy explores her  diagnosis at the age of 33. i became  quite a recluse in my 20s i stayed in  stayed at home pretty much for around  eight years since the documentary went  out i've had  messages off other women that i've  worked with  other tv shows that i've done just  saying  you know we really didn't understand you  we didn't get you you know being really  open and honest and saying now we  understand why dr sarah lister brooks  specializes in treating autism and says  the impact of a misdiagnosis can be  severe because of the stresses and  strains in sort of masking their  difficulties because this is something  we often see in the women and girls they  actually do sadly develop mental health  problems  because it's they become so exhausted at  trying to fit in and and to be pretend  to be something they're not i think it  needs to be  not just the frontline medical  professions but it's anyone that might  be involved in health social care  education need to know about autism  helen says the diagnosis changed her  life and she hopes that with more  understanding of the condition many more  women will feel they no longer have to  hide  katie barnfield sky news
 

